hexagon: add the int8 HMX inline asm macros for the v79 core

The HMX has a second data path that multiplies an unsigned 8-bit activation by
a signed 8-bit weight into an int32 accumulator. Its tile is 64 activation rows
by 32 channels against a 32 by 32 weight tile, thus it covers twice the rows of
the f16 tile, and the measured marginal cost of one issue is the same: 33.839
processor cycles against 34.735 for f16 (tools/hmx-bench, v79, 2026-09-20).
That is 2.05 times the multiply-accumulate rate.

This patch adds the macros only. No kernel uses them yet, thus the patch has no
effect on any result. It records what the core accepts, so that a later kernel
does not have to find it again.

Every form here comes from a probe of hexagon-llvm-mc at
-mcpu=hexagonv79 -mattr=+hvxv79,+hvx-length128b,+hmx, and each one also occurs
in the Qualcomm HexKL micro library built for the same core. The probe found the
complete weight type set of the core: hf, b, n (4-bit), c, sc, sm, f8, sbit and
ubit. Only ub assembles for an int activation, and only b for an int weight.

Two facts a kernel author must know, because they decide whether the int8 path
can carry a given weight format:

1. The int32 accumulator has no f16 and no f32 store. It leaves only as u8 or as
   u16. The full int32 value needs four stores of ":after:retain:cm.ub", each
   with its own scale in the bias register. The same measurement puts that read
   at approximately 711 processor cycles for one 64 by 32 tile, where the f16
   read costs nothing that the fit can see.

2. The accumulation cannot hold a scale. Thus the weight scale must be constant
   over the whole k range of one accumulation. A format with one scale for each
   32 k, for example Q8_0, needs one four-store read for each k tile, and that
   cost grows with k while the multiply gain does not. A format with one scale
   for each output row needs one read for the whole row.

The suffix order matters and the assembler is the only reference: ":deep:cm"
assembles and ":cm:deep" does not.

--- a/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hmx-utils.h
+++ b/ggml/src/ggml-hexagon/htp/hmx-utils.h
@@ -219,4 +219,91 @@
 #define HMX_CLRACC_F16() \
     "mxclracc.hf\n"
 
+// --- HMX inline asm macros for the int8 pipeline ---
+//
+// The HMX has a second data path that multiplies an unsigned 8-bit activation by a
+// signed 8-bit weight and accumulates into int32. One int8 tile is 64 activation rows
+// by 32 channels against a 32 by 32 weight tile, thus it covers twice the rows of the
+// f16 tile. The activation is unsigned and the weight is signed; no other pair of
+// types assembles for the v79 core.
+//
+// Each form here comes from a probe of hexagon-llvm-mc at
+// -mcpu=hexagonv79 -mattr=+hvxv79,+hvx-length128b,+hmx, and each one also occurs in
+// the Qualcomm HexKL micro library for the same core. Do not change a suffix or the
+// order of two suffixes without a new probe: ":deep:cm" assembles and ":cm:deep" does
+// not.
+//
+// The int32 accumulator has no f16 and no f32 store. It leaves only as u8 or as u16.
+// Thus a caller that wants the full int32 value reads it as four u8 byte planes:
+// four stores of ":after:retain:cm.ub", each with its own scale in the bias register,
+// with ":retain" holding the accumulator alive across the first three. The four
+// scales come from the conversion table that HMX_I32_READ_SCALES gives.
+//
+// The consequence for a quantized matmul: the scale of the weight must be constant
+// over the whole k dimension of one accumulation, because the accumulation cannot
+// hold a scale. A format with one scale for each 32 k, for example Q8_0, thus needs
+// one four-store read for each k tile. A format with one scale for each output row
+// needs one read for the whole row.
+
+#define HMX_I8_TILE_N_ROWS 64
+#define HMX_I8_TILE_N_COLS 32
+#define HMX_I8_TILE_N_ELMS 2048
+#define HMX_I8_WEIGHT_TILE_SIZE 1024
+
+// The range operand of the int8 activation load. The vendor library uses 0x1f for one
+// tile of 32 channels.
+#define HMX_I8_ACT_RANGE_ONE_TILE 0x1fu
+
+// The range operand of the int8 weight load for one 32 by 32 tile.
+#define HMX_I8_WT_RANGE_ONE_TILE 0x380u
+
+// Multiply one u8 activation tile by one s8 weight tile into the int32 accumulator.
+// The activation load is channel major, which is what the int8 path needs.
+#define HMX_LOAD_MPY_I8(act, wt, act_range, wt_range)      \
+    "{\n"                                                  \
+    "    activation.ub = mxmem(" act ", " act_range "):cm\n" \
+    "    weight.b = mxmem(" wt ", " wt_range ")\n"         \
+    "}\n"
+
+// The same multiplication with the deep flag, which chains the k tiles of one output
+// tile without a store between them.
+#define HMX_LOAD_MPY_DEEP_I8(act, wt, act_range, wt_range)       \
+    "{\n"                                                        \
+    "    activation.ub = mxmem(" act ", " act_range "):deep:cm\n" \
+    "    weight.b = mxmem(" wt ", " wt_range ")\n"               \
+    "}\n"
+
+// The same multiplication with a 4-bit weight tile. Two weights share one byte.
+#define HMX_LOAD_MPY_DEEP_I4(act, wt, act_range, wt_range)       \
+    "{\n"                                                        \
+    "    activation.ub = mxmem(" act ", " act_range "):deep:cm\n" \
+    "    weight.n = mxmem(" wt ", " wt_range ")\n"               \
+    "}\n"
+
+// Clear the int32 accumulator.
+#define HMX_CLRACC_I32() \
+    "mxclracc\n"
+
+// Load the output conversion table of the int path. The table is one word for each
+// output channel, not two, thus this is mxmem and not mxmem2.
+#define HMX_SET_BIAS_I32(scales) \
+    "bias = mxmem(" scales ")\n"
+
+// Store one byte plane of the int32 accumulator and keep the accumulator.
+#define HMX_STORE_PLANE_I32(out, range) \
+    "mxmem(" out ", " range "):after:retain:cm.ub = acc\n"
+
+// Store the last byte plane of the int32 accumulator and release it.
+#define HMX_STORE_PLANE_LAST_I32(out, range) \
+    "mxmem(" out ", " range "):after:cm.ub = acc\n"
+
+// The four scales of the conversion table that read the four byte planes of the int32
+// accumulator, as f16 bit patterns in the low half of each word. They are 2^9, 2^1,
+// 2^-7 and 2^-15, thus each plane is 8 bits lower than the one before it.
+#define HMX_I32_READ_SCALES { 0x00006000u, 0x00004000u, 0x00002000u, 0x00000000u }
+
+// Size in bytes of the HMX configuration region that the int32 read needs. The region
+// holds the four conversion tables at 1 KiB intervals.
+#define HMX_I32_READ_CONFIG_SIZE 0x4000u
+
 #endif // HMX_UTILS_H
